---
// The Build Games terms: what a sponsor buys, how the competition runs, and
// where the money goes. Public master document; the per-game brief (dates,
// judges, criteria, split) publishes on the game page before its build window
// opens and forms part of these terms. Flag-gated like the rest of the surface.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { buildGamesLive } from '../../lib/flags.js';
import { MIN_ENTRY_CENTS, MIN_TOPUP_CENTS, usd } from '../../lib/buildgames.js';

if (!buildGamesLive()) return new Response(null, { status: 404 });

const title = 'The Build Games · Terms';
const description = 'The terms for The Build Games: sponsor placements, free skill-judged entry, independent judging, and where the prize pool money goes.';
---

<Base {title} {description} hideSponsors={true} hideDigest={true}>
  <main class="wrap bgt">
    <h1>The Build Games · terms</h1>
    <p class="microlabel">
      last updated 2026-09-04 · operated by Faizan Ali, trading as vibecodeit
      (the "Operator", which includes any successor entity to which the Games are transferred)
    </p>

    <p class="bgt-banner">
      No purchase or payment of any kind is necessary to enter or win. Payment will not
      increase anyone's chances of winning. The Build Games is a competition of skill:
      winners are selected by an independent judging panel against published criteria,
      and chance plays no part. Void where prohibited or restricted by law.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>1 · What these terms cover</h2>
      <p>
        These terms govern The Build Games: the sponsor board at{' '}
        <a href="/thebuildgames">/thebuildgames</a> and the build competition it funds.
        By placing a bid, or by entering a game, you agree to them. Each game's page
        publishes its brief, dates, judges, criteria and prize split before that game's
        build window opens; the game page forms part of these terms. The main site has
        its own <a href="/terms">terms</a>. Where these terms are translated, the English
        version prevails.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Build Games is not sponsored, endorsed or administered by any platform, model
        provider or tool that entrants may use, nor by any board sponsor except as stated here.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>2 · Sponsors: what a bid buys</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A bid is a purchase of advertising.</strong> It buys a placement on the
          Build Games leaderboard: your icon, name, tagline and link, ranked by your
          cumulative cleared payments. If two sponsors have paid the same total, the
          earlier placement ranks higher. Advertising exposure is the entire product.
          Placements start at {usd(MIN_ENTRY_CENTS)} to join the board and{' '}
          {usd(MIN_TOPUP_CENTS)} to top up an existing placement; the current minimums are
          always shown on the bid form.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Nothing else comes with it.</strong> A placement confers no equity, no
          revenue share, no rights in any entry or its intellectual property, and no role
          in the Games beyond being visible.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sponsors cannot win.</strong> Sponsors and their owners, directors,
          employees and contractors are not eligible to enter or win any game they sponsor.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sponsors do not judge and cannot influence outcomes.</strong> No sponsor
          selects or scores entries, sees deliberations, or conditions payment on any
          outcome. Winners are determined solely by the independent judging panel.
          Attempting to influence judging forfeits the placement; the money stays in the pool.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Payments are final once the game opens.</strong> Placement fees are
          non-refundable from the moment the game's build window opens. If the Operator
          cancels a game before its build window opens, placement fees for that game are
          refunded in full.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Removal for abuse.</strong> The Operator may hold or remove a placement
          that is unlawful, deceptive, malicious, or harmful to visitors or to the Games'
          integrity. A placement removed for abuse is forfeited and the cleared money
          stays in the prize pool.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Taxes.</strong> Placement fees are exclusive of VAT, GST or sales taxes,
          which are the sponsor's responsibility where applicable.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>3 · The prize pool</h2>
      <p>
        The prize pool for each game is the total of sponsor placement fees received for
        that round, less any third-party payment processing fees (such as Stripe). The
        pool is paid to winners on the split published with the game's brief. The
        Operator takes no share of the pool: in every scenario, pool money is either
        paid to winners or returned to sponsors, never kept.
      </p>
      <p>
        The only costs deducted from the pool are third-party payment processing fees
        (such as Stripe). The Operator's own compensation, if any, is invoiced
        separately and is never taken from the pool.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a game is cancelled before its build window opens, sponsor fees are refunded
        and no prizes are due. If it is cancelled after submissions close, the entries
        received are judged and the pool is paid unless the Operator is legally prevented
        from doing so. In between, the Operator will choose reasonably between extending,
        judging what was received, or cancelling with refunds, and will publish its reasoning.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>4 · Builders: entering</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Entry is free, always.</strong> No purchase, payment or subscription is
          required to enter or win, and none will improve anyone's chances.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Eligibility.</strong> Entrants must be at least 18 (or the age of
          majority where they live, if higher), able to form a binding contract, and able
          to receive payment in their own legal name. Not eligible: sponsors of the game
          and their personnel, the judges, the Operator and its personnel, and immediate
          family or household members of any of those.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Where it runs.</strong> The Games are void where prohibited. They are not
          open to persons in jurisdictions under comprehensive sanctions, to persons on
          applicable sanctions lists, or to anyone the Operator cannot lawfully pay through
          its payment providers. By entering, you confirm entering and accepting a prize is
          lawful where you live.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The brief rules the build.</strong> Each game's brief states what must be
          built, the build window, the submission format and any game-specific rules. The
          work the judges evaluate must be performed during the build window. AI tools are
          permitted and expected; whatever tools are used, the entrant submits the entry as
          their own and is responsible for it. The Operator may require evidence (commit
          history, session logs) that the work was done in the window.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One entry per person or team</strong> per game, unless the brief says
          otherwise. Entries that are unlawful, infringing, malicious or deceptive are
          disqualified.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Proof.</strong> The Operator may require proof of eligibility and
          identity at any time; failing to provide it within 14 days of the request is
          grounds for disqualification.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>5 · Judging</h2>
      <p>
        Every eligible entry is judged by an independent judging panel against the criteria
        and weights published on the game page. The panel members, their bios and the full
        criteria are published before the game's build window opens. The Operator
        administers the game but does not score and holds no vote. Sponsors never judge,
        score, see deliberations, or influence outcomes in any way.
      </p>
      <p>
        Judges score independently first, then issue one collective final decision. A judge
        with a personal or financial connection to an entrant is recused from that entry.
        Judging involves expert qualitative judgment: the panel's decisions on scoring,
        ranking and winner selection are final and binding on all entrants and on the
        Operator, no correspondence or appeals are entered into, and individual scores and
        deliberations are not disclosed. Each entrant's sole entitlement in respect of
        judging is that the published process is applied in good faith.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing here excludes liability that cannot lawfully be excluded, and the finality
        of decisions does not apply to fraud, bad faith, or a material failure to follow
        the published judging process.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>6 · Prizes and payment</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The prize split for each game is published with its brief. Results and a short
          judges' citation for each winner are published on the game page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Verification before payment.</strong> Each provisional winner must,
          within 14 days of notification, verify their identity and eligibility, provide
          the applicable tax documentation and bank details in their own legal name, sign
          the winner agreement, and pass sanctions screening. A provisional winner is not
          a winner until verification completes.
        </li>
        <li>
          A share that is forfeited (failed verification, ineligibility, disqualification,
          or declining the prize) passes to the next highest-scoring eligible entry or is
          otherwise redistributed to winners. The pool is still paid out; the Operator
          keeps none of it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Prizes are stated gross and paid by bank transfer via the Operator's payment
          provider. All taxes on prizes are the winner's sole responsibility; where the
          Operator is legally required to withhold or report, it will do so and provide
          documentation. Prizes are not transferable.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>7 · Your work stays yours</h2>
      <p>
        Entrants retain full ownership of their entries and all intellectual property in
        them. By entering, you grant the Operator a non-exclusive, royalty-free licence to
        host, display and excerpt your entry (screenshots, video, code excerpts, write-up)
        to operate, judge, promote and archive the Games. You warrant the entry is your
        own work, made during the build window, that it infringes no third-party rights,
        complies with the licences of components it uses, and contains nothing unlawful
        or malicious.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>8 · Publicity</h2>
      <p>
        The winners list (name or handle, country, placement and prize amount) is
        published on the game page and remains available for at least 12 months. Winners
        agree to reasonable cooperation with the results announcement. Publicity beyond
        the winners list happens with the winner's consent.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>9 · Integrity and disqualification</h2>
      <p>
        The Operator may disqualify any entry or entrant, at any stage, for breach of
        these terms or the brief, plagiarism or work not built in the window, entry
        manipulation, attempting to influence judges or the Operator improperly, unlawful
        or malicious content, or conduct that harms the Games' integrity. If a violation
        is discovered after a prize is paid, the Operator may recover it as a debt.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>10 · Liability</h2>
      <p>
        The Games and the site are provided as is. To the fullest extent the law allows,
        the Operator, judges and sponsors are not liable for lost or corrupted
        submissions, outages of third-party platforms or AI services, acts of payment
        providers or banks, an entrant's own costs of entering, or any indirect or
        consequential loss. The Operator's total liability to any non-winning entrant is
        capped at USD 100; to any winner, at that winner's prize share; to any sponsor,
        at the fees that sponsor paid for the game. Nothing in these terms excludes
        liability that cannot lawfully be excluded, including for fraud, or for death or
        personal injury caused by negligence, and nothing affects non-excludable
        consumer rights.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>11 · Changes</h2>
      <p>
        The Operator may amend these terms or a game's brief to fix errors, comply with
        law, or preserve fairness. Material changes during an open game are notified to
        entrants on the game page. No change entitles the Operator to keep pool money.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>12 · Privacy, law, contact</h2>
      <p>
        Personal data is handled per the site's <a href="/privacy">privacy notice</a>.
        Winner verification documents are used only to administer the Games, shared only
        with payment and compliance providers and authorities as required, and never sold.
      </p>
      <p>
        Questions about these terms: email
        <a href="mailto:hello@vibecodeit.com">hello@vibecodeit.com</a>. If any provision is held
        invalid, the rest stands. Consumer rights that cannot be waived under your local law
        are unaffected.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .bgt {
    max-width: 720px;
    padding-top: 48px;
    padding-bottom: 96px;
  }

  .bgt h1 {
    font-size: 30px;
  }

  .bgt .microlabel {
    display: block;
    padding: 8px 0 20px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .bgt-banner {
    padding: 14px 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    background: var(--surface);
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .bgt section {
    padding-top: 26px;
  }

  .bgt h2 {
    font-size: 17px;
    padding-bottom: 8px;
  }

  .bgt p,
  .bgt li {
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  .bgt p + p {
    padding-top: 10px;
  }

  .bgt ul {
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 10px;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .bgt a {
    color: var(--primary);
  }
</style>
